codex
1. Performance hypothesis: `event-fast-par` was doing avoidable allocation in the cross-partition exchange loop. The hot path was flattening message arrays, rescanning buckets every flush round, and JSON-cloning primitive payloads that do not need deep copies.

2. What changed: I only edited the allowed target-engine files. In [engine-fast-par-host.js](/C:/Users/suzuk/AppData/Local/Temp/fact-sim-auto-patch/1773542386284-5500/js/app/engine-fast-par-host.js#L289) and [engine-fast-par-host.js](/C:/Users/suzuk/AppData/Local/Temp/fact-sim-auto-patch/1773542386284-5500/js/app/engine-fast-par-host.js#L627), I replaced `flatMap` plus bucket rescans with a reusable one-pass `bucketMessagesByPartition(...)` helper and reused bucket arrays across flush rounds. In [engine-fast-par-host.js](/C:/Users/suzuk/AppData/Local/Temp/fact-sim-auto-patch/1773542386284-5500/js/app/engine-fast-par-host.js#L8) and [engine-fast-par-worker.js](/C:/Users/suzuk/AppData/Local/Temp/fact-sim-auto-patch/1773542386284-5500/js/app/engine-fast-par-worker.js#L97), I added a primitive fast path to `cloneJson(...)` so `null`/number/string/boolean payloads skip the JSON round-trip. `dt`, `event`, `engine-test`, and harness files were untouched.

3. Focused verification I ran: `node --check` passed for `js/app/engine-fast-par-host.js`, `js/app/engine-fast-par-worker.js`, and `js/app/engine-fast-par-partitioner.js`. `fact-sim-ai` `engine_test` passed for both `quick` and `standard` with `engines=[dt,event,event-fast,event-fast-worker,event-fast-par]`, `strictFinalParity=true`, `reruns=2`, `stopOnFirstFailure=true` with 45/45 passing in each suite. On `parallel_benchmark` with `wallMs=500`, `event-fast-par` headless benchmark samples were `10020.256`, `9794.016`, and `9681.056`; against the provided baseline `9726.176`, that is a noisy range of `-0.46%` to `+3.02%`, with a 3-run average of `9831.776` or about `+1.09%`. I could not run `cd mcp && npm run check` or `npm run build` because this trimmed workspace does not contain an `mcp/` directory.

4. Residual risk: the speed gain is from reduced allocation/GC pressure, so benefit depends on how much cross-partition message traffic a graph produces. The 500 ms benchmark is noisy, and one sample dipped below baseline, so the improvement is plausible and measurable on average here, but not perfectly stable on every short run.
